## v0.3 Week 20 Mon — Orchestrator agent (multi-module project).
##
## Top-level entry-point that takes a natural-language feature
## spec + runs the full Architect → Coder → Code-reviewer chain
## end-to-end.  This is the v0.3 headline demo: a single
## `aislang chat full_design examples/agents/orchestrator/`
## invocation drives a 3-agent pipeline against the user's
## local Ollama (qwen3:8b + qwen2.5-coder:7b) + emits a
## structured design + Rust patch + reviewer verdict.
##
## ## Direct chat
##
## ```
## aislang chat full_design examples/agents/orchestrator/
## > Design a `dataset` block for declaring training data
##   (path + format fields, fits the existing v0.3 grammar).
## ```
##
## The CLI's pipeline-label dispatch (Week 17 Thu) drives the
## runtime patterns::orchestrator::run — each step's output
## feeds the next step's input verbatim.
##
## ## Coder ↔ Reviewer reflector loop (grammar layer)
##
## `loop.review_cycle` wraps `pipeline.coder_review_cycle` with a
## `condition` that re-runs the pipeline while the reviewer's
## verdict does NOT start with `ACCEPT`, hard-capped at 3 retries.
## This is the FIRST .ais-grammar-layer expression of the
## reflector pattern from Week 17 Fri — runtime dispatch via
## `loop`-block label is a follow-up (today Mon ships the
## grammar + a runtime integration test exercising the parsed
## loop AST through `patterns::reflector::run` with mocked
## sub-agent dispatch + the `max_iter` hard-cap honoured).
##
## ## Cross-module composition
##
## Sibling modules under `../architect/`, `../coder/`,
## `../code_reviewer/` are referenced via `use <module>.<label>`
## (Decision 44.1).  Each sibling is a self-contained
## `project { … }` block (the Week 19 single-file agents);
## the multi-module resolver merges all four projects' resource
## namespaces at validate time + this module's pipelines refer
## to the merged labels with the `use` prefix.

project "orchestrator-module" {
  budget {
    hard_cap_per_day = $2
    hard_cap_per_run = $0.50
  }

  ## Sequential chain — feature-spec → design → patch → verdict.
  ## Each step's output is the next step's input (Decision 44.5
  ## string-only handoff in v0.3; structured envelopes v0.5+).
  pipeline "full_design" {
    ## v0.3 Week 20 Tue: extended from 3 steps to 4 with the
    ## Tester leg appended.  Tester reads the Reviewer's
    ## ACCEPT-or-feedback verdict + the Coder's most-recent
    ## patch (threaded via the cumulative-input handoff) and
    ## emits a Test plan + canonical `cargo test` invocation.
    steps  = [use architect.architect, use coder.coder, use code_reviewer.code_reviewer, use tester.tester]
    input  = "natural-language feature spec for a new resource block"
    output = "Tester's Test plan + cargo test invocation + ACCEPT-or-FAIL verdict"
  }

  ## Inner pipeline for the reflector loop wrap below — just the
  ## Coder ↔ Reviewer two-step that the loop's condition checks
  ## on each iteration.
  pipeline "coder_review_cycle" {
    steps  = [use coder.coder, use code_reviewer.code_reviewer]
    input  = "Architect's 5-section design markdown"
    output = "Reviewer's verdict (ACCEPT or revision feedback)"
  }

  ## Grammar-layer reflector loop.  Condition uses MiniJinja's
  ## startswith filter (Decision 44.3) against the running output;
  ## re-runs until ACCEPT or max_iter hit.  Runtime dispatch via
  ## a `loop`-label CLI handler is a follow-up — today this block
  ## VALIDATES, PARSES, + flows through the resolver's lockfile
  ## emit, and a runtime integration test exercises the parsed
  ## Loop AST node through `patterns::reflector::run` with
  ## mocked sub-agent dispatch.
  loop "review_cycle" {
    body      = pipeline.coder_review_cycle
    condition = "{{ output | startswith('ACCEPT') | not }}"
    max_iter  = 3
  }
}
